   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to information to be provided\nby a veterinarian initially prescribing or dispensing a drug to a dog, cat, or\nrabbit; and to amend Buoy's law, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S7459",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7459-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6715, Ed L; amd §3, Chap 678 of 2022",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7459\n\nSPONSOR: MARTINEZ\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to information to be\nprovided by a veterinarian initially prescribing or dispensing a drug to\na dog, cat, or rabbit; and to amend Buoy's law, in relation to the\neffectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide technical amendments to chapter\n678 of the Laws of 2022.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. amends section 6715 of the education law to provide that this\nsection shall only apply to dogs, cats and rabbits. This section shall\nfurther limit the provisions of this section to only apply for medica-\ntions to be prescribed and dispensed outside of the office.  This\nsection also provides that any disclosures pursuant to subdivision one\nshall be provided in writing or orally and be noted in patient medical\nrecords.\n\nSection 2. amends section 3 of chapter 678 of the laws of 2022 to change\nthe effective date from 180 days to 18 months.\n\nSection 3. is the enacting clause\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThese technical amendments are based on concerns expressed by the State\nEducation Department (SED) on the implementation of the current chapter.\nMedications administered outside of the office should require disclosure\nin the event that a side effect occurs. There are no trained veterinari-\nans with the patient outside of the office and the disclosure of this\ninformation could be vital in ensuring pet safety. Medication informa-\ntion should also only be administered upon initial prescription to avoid\nany potential redundancy or confusion.\n\nThe current chapter would also require disclosure of medicinal informa-\ntion to patients of all animals receiving veterinary care.  This created\nan issue due to the manufacturer of these medications not always provid-\ning veterinarians the information on the medications prescribed to the\npatients. This is especially common in animals not deemed traditional\n\"companion animals\", which is the category that dogs, cats and rabbits\nall fall into. This legislation would limit the provisions related to\ndisclosure to such \"companion\" animals.\n\nThe technical amendments made also took into consideration the effective\ndate for the chaptered legislation. Extending the time period from 180\ndays to 18 months ensures that SED has enough time to properly write and\nreview regulations on provisions related to this chapter.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided, however, that section\none of this act shall take effect on the same date and in the same\nmanner as section 2 of chapter 678 of the laws of 2022, takes effect.\n",
